static blocks gets executed even before main function in a class
In older versions only static block without main func works but in later versions it throws error
static arrays should be initialized inside a static block

formula to find day of the week for any date:
String []DAYS;
            DAYS = new String[]{"SUNDAY","MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY","SATURDAY"};
        
        day += ((month < 3) ? year-- : (year - 2));
        int dayOfWeek = ((((23 * month) / 9) + day + 4 + (year / 4)) - (year / 100) + (year / 400)) % 7;
        return DAYS[dayOfWeek];



Aggregation: means an object containing another object(Has-a relationship)

Compile Time Error is better than Run Time Error. So, java compiler renders compiler time error if you declare the same method having same parameters and only with change in return-type.

Rule: If parent class method is declared protected, the child class’ same method cannot be declared empty.
Rule: If the superclass method does not declare an exception, subclass overridden method cannot declare the checked exception.
Exceptions:
Checked exceptions – checked at compiletime (e.g. IOException, SQLException etc)
Unchecked exceptions – checked at runtime (e.g. ArithmeticException, NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException etc)
Error is irrecoverable e.g. OutOfMemoryError, VirtualMachineError, AssertionError

throw is to throw an exception
throws is to declare an exceptions

In a class, public void finalize() method is used to perform clean up processing just before object is garbage collected

If the superclass method does not declare an exception
        ◦ If the superclass method does not declare an exception, subclass overridden method cannot declare the checked exception but it can declare unchecked exception.
    • If the superclass method declares an exception
        ◦ If the superclass method declares an exception, subclass overridden method can declare same, subclass exception or no exception but cannot declare parent exception.


Super is used to call immediate parent class attributes
super() calls immediate parent class constructor

instance initializer block : gets called everytime when obj is created
class Person {
int data;
{ data =5; System.out.println(“instance init block invoked: “ + data); }
static {  System.out.println(“static block invoked: “); }
}
public static void main(String []args) {
	Person p1 = new Person();
	Person p2 = new Person();
}
Output:
static block invoked
instance init block invoked: 5
instance init block invoked: 5

final: 
final variables cannot be changed
final methods cannot be overridden but can be inherited
final class cannot be extended
Can we initialize blank final variable?
Yes, but only in constructor

A method is overridden, not the data members, so runtime polymorphism can't be achieved by data members.

Dog d=(Dog)new Animal();  
    1. //Compiles successfully but ClassCastException is thrown at runtime 

interface has static constants and abstract methods.
It is used to achieve abstraction and multiple inheritance
multiple inheritance is not supported in the case of class because of ambiguity. However, it is supported in case of an interface because there is no ambiguity. It is because its implementation is provided by the implementation class.
Since java 8, interface supports implementation of method through default and static methods only
An interface can have another interface which is known as a nested interface.

The precision may differ from platform to platform that is why java programming language have provided the strictfp keyword, so that you get same result on every platform.

String:
String str = "GeeksForGeeks"; 
char[] ch = str.toCharArray(); 

String str = "GeeksForGeeks"; 
char[] ch = new char[str.length()]; 
Character []chr = new Character[str.length()];
for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) { 
    ch[i] = str.charAt(i); 
	chr[i] = str.charAt(i);
} 

String is non-modifiable inplace whereas StringBuffer and StringBuilder provides inplace modification
Reverse a string:
StringBuffer buffer = new StringBuffer(word);
System.out.println(buffer.reverse().toString()+ " ");

Composition:
One class holding another class object as its data member is called composition.


